## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of memory hierarchy and cache effects, which are crucial for writing high-performance, low-latency code in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- **False sharing**: When two threads modify variables that share the same cache line, even if they access different variables, the cache coherence protocol forces invalidation and reloads
- **Cache lines**: Typically 64 bytes on modern CPUs; data is transferred between main memory and CPU caches in these blocks
- **MESI protocol**: Cache coherence protocol (Modified, Exclusive, Shared, Invalid) that ensures consistency but introduces overhead when false sharing occurs
- **Memory alignment and padding**: Restructuring data to occupy separate cache lines eliminates false sharing at the cost of increased memory usage
- **pthreads and barriers**: Lightweight threading with synchronization primitives to coordinate threads and measure timing accurately

## Code Example
```c
// Variant A: no padding (false sharing occurs)
typedef struct { long v0; long v1; } counters_t;

// Variant B: 64-byte padding per counter (no false sharing)
typedef struct { 
    long v0; 
    char pad0[64 - sizeof(long)]; 
    long v1; 
    char pad1[64 - sizeof(long)]; 
} counters_padded_t;
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design robust, low-latency IPC primitives under NUMA-aware multi-producer/multi-consumer contention with precise memory ordering requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free design, with ABA avoidance via per-slot sequence counters
- NUMA-aware memory placement to minimize cross-socket memory traffic
- Shadow consumer tail optimization to reduce remote cache-line access
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Memory ordering semantics (acquire/release, memory_order_acq_rel)

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t sequence;
    char data[56];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    slot_t slots[N] __attribute__((aligned(64)));
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
    volatile uint64_t shadow_tail;
} mpmc_queue_t;

static inline int enqueue(mpmc_queue_t* q, const void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(s->data, data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}

static inline int dequeue(mpmc_queue_t* q, void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->head, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot + 1) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(data, s->data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + N, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}
```

